    <item>
  <title>Test of Longhorn Dam Floodgate Warning Sirens Scheduled for September 15 </title>
  <link>https://www.austintexas.gov/water/news/test-longhorn-dam-floodgate-warning-sirens-scheduled-september-15</link>
  <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AUSTIN –&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Austin Water will conduct a test of the floodgate warning sirens at Longhorn Dam on Tuesday, September 15, 2026, at&amp;nbsp;10 a.m.&amp;nbsp;Residents and visitors in the surrounding area may hear the siren during the test. No emergency is occurring and no action is&amp;nbsp;required&amp;nbsp;from the public. The siren&amp;nbsp;test&amp;nbsp;will sound,&amp;nbsp;one time,&amp;nbsp;continuously for&amp;nbsp;five&amp;nbsp;minutes.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Pedestrian&amp;nbsp;traffic&amp;nbsp;will not be&amp;nbsp;permitted&amp;nbsp;on&amp;nbsp;the&amp;nbsp;Longhorn Dam section of sidewalk&amp;nbsp;along&amp;nbsp;Pleasant Valley&amp;nbsp;Road&amp;nbsp;during the siren test.&amp;nbsp;There will be no&amp;nbsp;impact&amp;nbsp;to&amp;nbsp;vehicle traffic&amp;nbsp;and no impact&amp;nbsp;to&amp;nbsp;the Hike and Bike Trail&amp;nbsp;or parkland.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The siren test is part of Austin Water’s ongoing emergency preparedness program.&amp;nbsp;The&amp;nbsp;test at the dam will not change the position or status of the floodgates.&amp;nbsp;It is intended to test the operation of the sirens and&amp;nbsp;confirm the range and coverage&amp;nbsp;of the sound.&amp;nbsp;The test will provide personnel with an opportunity to&amp;nbsp;monitor&amp;nbsp;and verify sound levels at various points along Lady Bird Lake and the Colorado River.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;If this was&amp;nbsp;not a test,&amp;nbsp;in the event of&amp;nbsp;an actual&amp;nbsp;severe weather&amp;nbsp;emergency&amp;nbsp;requiring the release of storm runoff, the Longhorn Dam floodgate warning sirens would sound&amp;nbsp;before&amp;nbsp;the floodgates were opened. Increased flows, strong moving&amp;nbsp;currents&amp;nbsp;and unsafe water conditions&amp;nbsp;would&amp;nbsp;be present&amp;nbsp;and recreational swimming, boating,&amp;nbsp;kayaking&amp;nbsp;and other water activities should be avoided.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;“This test is an important part of maintaining our emergency preparedness&amp;nbsp;and helps us reach the community quickly in an emergency,” said Stephanie Sue, Austin Water&amp;nbsp;Assistant&amp;nbsp;Director of&amp;nbsp;Operations.&amp;nbsp;“We’re grateful for&amp;nbsp;the public’s understanding as we test the sirens and strengthen&amp;nbsp;our systems&amp;nbsp;and emergency communication tools.”&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Modernization of the floodgate warning sirens began in&amp;nbsp;2026, with final installation completed in August 2026.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About Longhorn Dam&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Austin Water manages the operation of Longhorn Dam for the City of Austin. Since taking over operations in&amp;nbsp;2018, Austin Water has conducted extensive maintenance and improvements to Longhorn Dam’s&amp;nbsp;controls&amp;nbsp;as well as the mechanical systems that move the various gates controlling water flow.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Visit&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;a class="ck-page_link" href="https://austintexas.gov/longhorndam"&gt;&lt;span&gt;austintexas.gov/longhorndam&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;for more information about Longhorn Dam operations.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
  <pubDate>September 8, 2026</pubDate>
    <dc:creator/>
    <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.austintexas.gov/143971</guid>
    </item>
<item>
  <title>Texas Chapter of American Planning Association Awards Gold to Equity-Based Preservation Plan</title>
  <link>https://www.austintexas.gov/planning/news/texas-chapter-american-planning-association-awards-gold-equity-based-preservation</link>
  <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The American Planning Association Texas Chapter has recognized the City of Austin’s &lt;/span&gt;&lt;a href="https://gcc02.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fus.list-manage.com%2FeGPMcUjeV7b%3Fe%3D31c1f17024%26c2id%3D75ddfe5c56e9672ead9b8f00912a8743&amp;amp;data=05%7C02%7CCaleb.Pritchard%40austintexas.gov%7C0ef461f96b5c44044a9e08df0de0e3b7%7C5c5e19f6a6ab4b45b1d0be4608a9a67f%7C0%7C0%7C639244931918610183%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;amp;sdata=4Hv9bQrZxRyn%2Bme74h88SsamDr23GPbmy9ZfX%2BDmtEk%3D&amp;amp;reserved=0"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Equity-Based Preservation Plan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span&gt; as a Gold Winner in the Historic Preservation category of its 2026 Texas Planning Awards.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;Led by Austin Planning’s Historic Preservation Office, the Plan is a community-authored roadmap that harnesses historic preservation to support a more equitable, inclusive, and sustainable Austin.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;“We’re grateful for this recognition and we’re proud to share it with the many community members who shaped the Plan,” said Austin Planning Director Andrea Bates. “More than 2,500 Austinites contributed to its creation through public meetings, surveys, and events, alongside the Historic Landmark Commission and the Preservation Plan Working Group. This award reflects our collective commitment to Austin’s heritage as we plan for the future.”&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;The Plan’s 14 goals and 107 recommendations aim to safeguard cultural assets like legacy businesses and murals, use preservation tools to slow displacement of longtime residents, and promote environmental sustainability. It provides guidance on identifying and preserving important places, supporting property owners, streamlining regulatory review, and strengthening protections for historic resources.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;The APA Texas Planning Awards Program recognizes outstanding planning achievements and leadership across the state. Nominees were evaluated on originality and innovation, community engagement, implementation and effectiveness, quality, and promotion of planning. The Equity-Based Preservation Plan joins Austin Watershed Protection’s Rain to River Plan and The Guide to Activate Undeveloped Parks of Austin as 2026 City of Austin Gold Winners.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;To read more about the 2026 Texas Planning Award categories and recipients, visit &lt;/span&gt;&lt;a href="https://gcc02.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fus.list-manage.com%2F16aMlnDAnMb%3Fe%3D31c1f17024%26c2id%3D75ddfe5c56e9672ead9b8f00912a8743&amp;amp;data=05%7C02%7CCaleb.Pritchard%40austintexas.gov%7C0ef461f96b5c44044a9e08df0de0e3b7%7C5c5e19f6a6ab4b45b1d0be4608a9a67f%7C0%7C0%7C639244931918631898%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;amp;sdata=LdewZ83Ez9izmuIhL66F%2B9rS0jv%2BFWcanyJHGFv4xhM%3D&amp;amp;reserved=0"&gt;&lt;span&gt;APA-Texas' website&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p class="text-align-center"&gt;&lt;span&gt;###&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
  <pubDate>September 8, 2026</pubDate>
    <dc:creator/>
    <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.austintexas.gov/143906</guid>
    </item>
<item>
  <title>Labor Day Personal Watercraft Ban on Lake Austin</title>
  <link>https://www.austintexas.gov/police/news/labor-day-personal-watercraft-ban-lake-austin</link>
  <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The Austin Police Department (APD) Lake Patrol Unit will be enforcing the City Ordinance that prohibits the use and operation of personal watercraft, wet bikes, motorized surfboards, and similar devices (designed to be operated by a person sitting, standing, or kneeling on the vessel), on Lake Austin. This ban will go into effect beginning at sunset on Friday, September 4, 2026, and ending on Tuesday, September 8, 2026, at sunrise, as outlined in Ordinance 8-5-81. Non-motorized devices such as kayaks, canoes, and paddleboards can still be used along with watercraft designed for the conventional manner of sitting or standing in the vessel.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The annual ban on personal watercraft is necessary to ensure the safety of many people using the lake and parks over the Labor Day Holiday. This ban only applies to Lake Austin.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Police will patrol Lake Austin this weekend and urge people using waterways to follow the Texas Water Safety Act and City of Austin boating laws and ordinances. Police urge all boaters to practice safe boating and to call 9-1-1 for all reckless operation of boats or emergencies.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The Austin Parks and Recreation Department and the Austin Police Department want the public to enjoy the parks and lakes but urge people to make this a safe holiday.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;City Ordinance:&amp;nbsp; 8-5-81 WATERCRAFT AND OTHER MOTORIZED CRAFT ON LAKE AUSTIN.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; (A)&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; A person may not use or possess a jet ski, wet bike, motorized surfboard, or similar device on Lake Austin:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; (1)&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; from sunset on the Friday before Memorial Day until sunrise on the Tuesday after Memorial Day;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; (2)&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; from sunset on the Friday before Labor Day until sunrise on the Tuesday after Labor Day; or&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; (3)&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; from sunset on July 3 until sunrise on July 5.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; (B)&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; A person operating a motorboat on Lake Austin shall stay on the right side of the lake, except when assisting a downed water skier.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; (C)&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; A person may not operate a watercraft on Lake Austin at a speed that is greater than reasonable and prudent under the existing circumstances.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
  <pubDate>September 4, 2026</pubDate>
    <dc:creator/>
    <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.austintexas.gov/143776</guid>
    </item>
<item>
  <title>City of Austin Closures for Labor Day 2026</title>
  <link>https://www.austintexas.gov/communications/news/city-austin-closures-labor-day-2026</link>
  <description>&lt;p paraeid="{0b2787bb-ce4f-42fa-96ff-fa94ab320249}{243}" paraid="198579086"&gt;City of Austin administrative offices and other municipal facilities will be closed Monday, September 7, in observance of Labor Day. This includes the Municipal and Community courts, Austin Animal Shelter, libraries, museums, and cultural, recreation and senior centers. Normal business hours will resume Tuesday, September 8.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The following City services will continue to operate as normal:&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;City of Austin Utilities Payments –&lt;/strong&gt; Customers can make payments online at &lt;a href="https://coautilities.com/wps/wcm/connect/occ/coa/home"&gt;coautilities.com&lt;/a&gt; or by calling 1-833-375-4949. In-person payments are accepted at most H-E-B, Fiesta Mart, Randall’s and Walmart stores.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Austin Resource Recovery Curbside Collections –&lt;/strong&gt; Curbside collections will follow regular schedules and will not be affected by the holiday. Customers can check their collection schedule in the Austin Recycles app or at &lt;a href="https://www.austintexas.gov/services/view-your-recycling-composting-and-trash-schedule"&gt;austintexas.gov/myschedule&lt;/a&gt;. The &lt;a href="https://www.austintexas.gov/resource-recovery/locations/recycle-and-reuse-drop-center"&gt;Recycle and Reuse Center&lt;/a&gt; will be closed, but &lt;a href="https://www.austintexas.gov/resource-recovery/programs/yard-trimmings-and-large-brush-drop"&gt;Austin Water’s Hornsby Bend Biosolids Management Plant&lt;/a&gt; will be open regular hours (8 a.m. - 3 p.m.) for yard trimmings and large brush drop-off.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Austin Parks and Recreation –&lt;/strong&gt; City parks, golf courses, cemeteries, splash pads and year-round pools will be open. The Garrison, Givens, Mabel Davis, Sheffield Northwest and Walnut Creek seasonal pools will also be open. Offices, recreation centers and senior centers will be closed. Hours vary by location so please visit the &lt;a href="https://www.austintexas.gov/department/parks-and-recreation"&gt;Parks and Recreation&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://www.austintexas.gov/page/pard-facilities-closures"&gt;Park Closures&lt;/a&gt; pages for up-to-date details.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The City recommends utilizing the following public resources, if needed, during the Labor Day holiday.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li paraeid="{0b2787bb-ce4f-42fa-96ff-fa94ab320249}{243}" paraid="198579086"&gt;Report power outages at &lt;a href="https://outagemap.austinenergy.com/"&gt;outagemap.austinenergy.com&lt;/a&gt; or by calling 512-322-9100.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li paraeid="{0b2787bb-ce4f-42fa-96ff-fa94ab320249}{243}" paraid="198579086"&gt;For non-emergency issues or to submit service requests, call 3-1-1, download the &lt;a href="https://austintexas.us5.list-manage.com/track/click?u=1861810ce1dca1a4c1673747c&amp;amp;id=84fc8b66e4&amp;amp;e=b3cea23241"&gt;Austin 3-1-1 mobile app&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://austin-cwiprod.motorolasolutions.com/"&gt;submit the request online&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li paraeid="{0b2787bb-ce4f-42fa-96ff-fa94ab320249}{243}" paraid="198579086"&gt;Report water or wastewater emergencies by calling 512-972-1000.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li paraeid="{0b2787bb-ce4f-42fa-96ff-fa94ab320249}{243}" paraid="198579086"&gt;Library resources can be accessed at the &lt;a href="https://library.austintexas.gov/"&gt;Austin Public Library website&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;</description>
  <pubDate>September 4, 2026</pubDate>
    <dc:creator/>
    <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.austintexas.gov/143766</guid>
    </item>
<item>
  <title>APD Detectives Search for a Suspect in Connection to an Auto Theft Investigation</title>
  <link>https://www.austintexas.gov/police/news/apd-detectives-search-suspect-connection-auto-theft-investigation-0</link>
  <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"&gt;Case:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;26-2420692&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Date:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;August 29, 2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Location: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;2300 block of Carson Ridge&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;The Austin Police (APD) Auto Theft Unit is requesting assistance in identifying a suspect involved in an auto theft that occurred in the 2300 block of Carson Ridge.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;On August 29, 2026, at approximately 3:07 a.m., video surveillance from a neighboring business captured the suspect operating the stolen motorcycle and traveling toward East Ben White Boulevard. &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The suspect is described as:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;div data-entity-type="media" data-entity-uuid="4e45a606-4bb1-4600-b5ff-eb587b56d563" data-embed-button="media_entity_embed" data-entity-embed-display="view_mode:media.medium" class="align-center embedded-entity ckeditor-image" data-langcode="und" data-entity-embed-display-settings="[]" embed_alt="Pictures of suspect"&gt;&lt;article class="media media--type-acquia-dam-image-asset media--view-mode-medium"&gt;
  
      
            &lt;div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;img src="https://austin.widen.net/content/975a6732-2ebe-43d5-bd28-6cc12ae72bda/web/Police%20-%20Web_Suspect%20pictures%2026-2420692.png?w=600&amp;amp;itok=C2o7H2MD" width="600" alt="Pictures of suspect"&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
      
  &lt;/article&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;White male&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Early to mid 30s&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Wavy brown hair&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Medium build&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Between 5'8" - 5"10" tall&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Last seen wearing a black long-sleeved t-shirt with "well boss" logo on&amp;nbsp;the back, blue jeans, and black tennis shoes.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The stolen vehicle is described as:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;div data-entity-type="media" data-entity-uuid="fcda41c5-25a1-4752-8a1b-fc9d67fa26b6" data-embed-button="media_entity_embed" data-entity-embed-display="view_mode:media.medium" class="align-center embedded-entity ckeditor-image" data-langcode="und" data-entity-embed-display-settings="[]" embed_alt="Stolen motorcycle"&gt;&lt;article class="media media--type-acquia-dam-image-asset media--view-mode-medium"&gt;
  
      
            &lt;div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;img src="https://austin.widen.net/content/7499a47d-e8c3-44d8-aa43-b4d6bf491faf/web/Police%20-%20Web_Stolen%20motorcycle%20-26-2420692.png?w=600&amp;amp;itok=0JpNCGmn" width="600" alt="Stolen motorcycle"&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
      
  &lt;/article&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;2001&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Moto Guzzi&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Pearl-white color&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Plate: TX 016C9Y&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Anyone with information is asked to contact&amp;nbsp;the APD Auto Theft Unit at (512) 974-5265 &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;or via email at ATIP@austintexas.gov. You may submit your tip anonymously through the Capital Area Crime Stoppers Program by visiting&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;a href="http://austincrimestoppers.org/" data-extlink title="(opens in a new window)"&gt;austincrimestoppers.org&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;or calling 512-472-8477.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
  <pubDate>September 3, 2026</pubDate>
    <dc:creator/>
    <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.austintexas.gov/143716</guid>
    </item>
<item>
  <title>DNA Evidence Links Two 1999 and 2000 Sexual Assaults to Same Suspect</title>
  <link>https://www.austintexas.gov/police/news/dna-evidence-links-two-1999-and-2000-sexual-assaults-same-suspect</link>
  <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The Austin Police (APD) has closed two sexual assault investigations dating back more than 25 years after DNA evidence identified the same suspect in both cases and ultimately led to his arrest and conviction.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;On September 9, 1999, a woman was at the Capital Metro Pavilion Park and Ride in the 12400 block of Research Boulevard after arriving on a northbound Leander bus. At approximately 9:15 to 9:30 p.m., she encountered an unknown man who approached her while she was sitting on a bench. As she got up to walk toward a nearby convenience store, the man physically assaulted her and attempted to sexually assault her. When the woman screamed, the man fled southbound. APD responded and collected forensic evidence during the investigation, documented under APD Case #1999-922521628.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;Less than five months later, on February 8, 2000, a second woman was returning to her residence in the 9000 block of Northgate Boulevard at approximately noon. As she opened the door to her apartment, an unknown man asked her for assistance. She allowed him inside, at which point he violently attacked and restrained her and attempted to sexually assault her. The woman fought back, and the man fled. APD responded and collected forensic evidence from the scene, documented under APD Case #2000-920390716.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;Investigators continued working both cases over the years. On April 3, 2000, APD received notification from the Texas Department of Public Safety (TxDPS) that DNA evidence collected from both incidents matched the same unknown male. Despite continued investigative efforts and the development of leads, the identity of the suspect remained unknown for more than two decades.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;On October 11, 2023, APD received notification from TxDPS that the DNA profile had been identified as belonging to Michael Lance Gamez.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;In February 2024, APD Detectives William Costello and Grace Wolfsbane located Gamez, obtained a DNA search warrant and conducted an interview. A DNA sample obtained from Gamez was submitted to the TxDPS Crime Laboratory for comparison with the male DNA evidence collected in the two unsolved sexual assault cases.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;On October 30, 2024, the TxDPS Crime Laboratory confirmed that Gamez was the source of the DNA evidence collected from the two cases.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;On November 15, 2024, Gamez was arrested and charged in connection with the attacks on the two women. The charges included Aggravated Kidnapping, a first-degree felony; Attempted Aggravated Sexual Assault, a second-degree felony; and Attempted Sexual Assault, a third-degree felony.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;On May 20, 2026, Gamez pleaded guilty to one count of Attempted Aggravated Sexual Assault, a second-degree felony, and requested sentencing by a judge.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;On August 3, 2026, the Honorable Chantal Eldridge of the 331st District Court sentenced Gamez to 15 years in the Texas Department of Criminal Justice.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;div data-entity-type="media" data-entity-uuid="348bf3f8-23aa-4278-82a6-2b2fe9c0a928" data-embed-button="media_entity_embed" data-entity-embed-display="view_mode:media.small" class="align-center embedded-entity ckeditor-image" data-langcode="und" data-entity-embed-display-settings="[]" embed_alt="Picture of suspect"&gt;&lt;article class="media media--type-acquia-dam-image-asset media--view-mode-small"&gt;
  
      
            &lt;div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;img src="https://austin.widen.net/content/b5b61813-c9a7-4cc4-8af6-c2f42afa3cb5/web/Police%20-%20Web_Gamez,%20Michael%20Lance%20(51%20yoa)%201999-922521628.jpg?itok=wO4EfmHk" width="300" alt="Picture of suspect"&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
      
  &lt;/article&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The resolution of these cases highlights the importance of preserving forensic evidence and continuing to pursue investigative leads, even when a case remains unsolved for many years. DNA evidence collected more than two decades ago ultimately connected two separate sexual assaults and provided investigators with the information needed to identify and prosecute the person responsible.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
  <pubDate>September 2, 2026</pubDate>
    <dc:creator/>
    <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.austintexas.gov/143701</guid>
    </item>
<item>
  <title>Community Invited to Fiesta de Salud Health Fair Sept. 19 </title>
  <link>https://www.austintexas.gov/health/news/community-invited-fiesta-de-salud-health-fair-sept-19</link>
  <description>&lt;p paraid="51679548" paraeid="{282f4e84-6389-48a5-b182-99c0673125be}{12}"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span data-contrast="auto" xml:lang="EN-US" lang="EN-US"&gt;Public Health Fair Attendance Increases from Past Years.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span data-ccp-props="{}"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p paraid="51679548" paraeid="{282f4e84-6389-48a5-b182-99c0673125be}{12}"&gt;&lt;span data-contrast="auto" xml:lang="EN-US" lang="EN-US"&gt;The City of Austin and Austin Public Health (APH) invite everyone to the FREE Fiesta de Salud Health Fair on Saturday, September 19, from 11 a.m. to 2 p.m. at the Emma S. Barrientos Mexican American Cultural Center (600 River Street, Austin, TX 78701).&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p paraid="958905708" paraeid="{282f4e84-6389-48a5-b182-99c0673125be}{14}"&gt;&lt;span data-contrast="auto" xml:lang="EN-US" lang="EN-US"&gt;The annual event aims to inspire our communities to live well and improve access to health services and resources, all while having fun. With a spotlight on Hispanic/Latino culture, organizers will provide live entertainment, community resources, health screenings, and more. &amp;nbsp;Over the last three years, more than 800 individuals have received services.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p paraid="439568863" paraeid="{282f4e84-6389-48a5-b182-99c0673125be}{16}"&gt;&lt;span data-contrast="auto" xml:lang="EN-US" lang="EN-US"&gt;“Fiesta de Salud feels right at home at the Mexican American Cultural Center, where art, history, and Austinites can come together in a celebration of health,” said Austin Public Health Director Adrienne Sturrup. “Providing free health services in a festive way to the community reduces the barriers to living a healthy life, something we all deserve.”&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p paraid="779387398" paraeid="{282f4e84-6389-48a5-b182-99c0673125be}{18}"&gt;&lt;span data-contrast="auto" xml:lang="EN-US" lang="EN-US"&gt;In the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;a href="https://austin.widen.net/view/pdf/qcjtqwfkda/2025-Chronic-Disease-Report-Final-5.15.2025.pdf?t.download=true"&gt;&lt;span data-contrast="none" xml:lang="EN-US" lang="EN-US" data-ccp-charstyle="Hyperlink"&gt;2025 Chronic Disease Report&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span data-contrast="auto" xml:lang="EN-US" lang="EN-US"&gt;, social and economic factors largely contribute to&amp;nbsp;a high diabetes rate in the Hispanic and Black population. Also, due to the same factors, Hispanics have a higher rate of dying of chronic liver disease with 68% of Hispanic adults overweight or obese.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p paraid="738692707" paraeid="{282f4e84-6389-48a5-b182-99c0673125be}{20}"&gt;&lt;span data-contrast="auto" xml:lang="EN-US" lang="EN-US"&gt;Fiesta de Salud is one of three annual events designed to encourage our community to take proactive steps to manage their health. This is a FREE health screening event. Everyone is welcome, regardless of insurance.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span data-ccp-props="{}"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
  <pubDate>September 2, 2026</pubDate>
    <dc:creator/>
    <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.austintexas.gov/143676</guid>
    </item>
<item>
  <title>Austin's Art in Public Places Artwork "Absolute Equality: Unity Tunnel" Named 2026 CODAworx Winner for Best Collaboration</title>
  <link>https://www.austintexas.gov/communications/news/austins-art-public-places-artwork-absolute-equality-unity-tunnel-named-2026</link>
  <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Austin Arts, Culture, Music and Entertainment’s (ACME) &lt;strong&gt;“Absolute Equality: Unity Tunnel,”&lt;/strong&gt; an iconic Art in Public Places (AIPP) work by Texas artist &lt;strong&gt;Reginald Adams&lt;/strong&gt;, has been selected as the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;a href="https://gcc02.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fus.list-manage.com%2F14rxjyw5YJA%3Fe%3De70aa4c24c%26c2id%3Dff4227888f0dfd05153fcefbfcf6992e&amp;amp;data=05%7C02%7Cmedia%40austintexas.gov%7Cdd8f198fe3ca4f8bf65008df0872e7cb%7C5c5e19f6a6ab4b45b1d0be4608a9a67f%7C0%7C0%7C639238961968160460%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;amp;sdata=7Jecziz7iaZaK0d3%2Ft9%2FPNIy5fyMtCTdTsxtD5TGn%2F8%3D&amp;amp;reserved=0"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2026 CODAworx Winner for Best Collaboration&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;The &lt;/span&gt;&lt;a href="https://gcc02.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fus.list-manage.com%2FmZD2RTrNNnV%3Fe%3De70aa4c24c%26c2id%3Dff4227888f0dfd05153fcefbfcf6992e&amp;amp;data=05%7C02%7Cmedia%40austintexas.gov%7Cdd8f198fe3ca4f8bf65008df0872e7cb%7C5c5e19f6a6ab4b45b1d0be4608a9a67f%7C0%7C0%7C639238961968169704%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;amp;sdata=G%2B%2FLjjmZsTCKV9UKcAqP4bsneCctySTry%2FEIjymvRV4%3D&amp;amp;reserved=0"&gt;&lt;span&gt;CODAawards&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span&gt; honor excellence in commissioned art around the world. This year’s jurors were particularly impressed by the collaborative spirit behind “Unity Tunnel,” describing the project as “the very definition of collaboration.” This recognition celebrates the partnership among AIPP, Adams, Austin Transportation and Public Works, Austin Capital Delivery Services, HDR Inc., Jay-Reese Contractors, Inc., Austin Parks and Recreation, local non-profit The Trail Conservancy, and the over 250 Austin residents who shaped every stage of its development.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;From CODAworx - “This year's competition drew 302 submissions from 19 countries, collectively representing more than $123 million in commissions to artists. A distinguished panel of jurors from the fields of art, architecture, and design selected winners across twelve categories spanning sculpture, murals, light art, and beyond — recognizing the creative professionals whose work transforms environments, shapes experiences, and strengthens community connection."&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h5&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A Triumph of Community Partnership&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;“Unity Tunnel” was created through an extensive community engagement process designed to ensure that Austin residents had a meaningful role in shaping the artwork. &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;From early community surveys and engagements to hands-on workshops at the Mendez Recreation Center and The Mosaic Workshop, AIPP and Adams worked alongside community members to develop “Unity Tunnel” with broad and meaningful public participation.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;The project took innovative approaches to building trust for community participation. Community activities included sound baths, guided meditation, and quiet reflection, helped ensure that voices across all ages and backgrounds were included, inspiring community pride and shaping an artwork meant to endure for generations.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h5&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A Celebrated Moment for Austin’s Public Art Program&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;“This recognition shines a light on the power of collaboration in public art,” said &lt;strong&gt;Angela Means, ACME Director.&lt;/strong&gt; “’Unity Tunnel’ is a testament to what’s possible when artists, residents, and City partners come together with imagination, dedication, and heart.”&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;CODAworx is scheduled to publicly announce the 2026 award winners on &lt;strong&gt;Monday, August 31,&lt;/strong&gt; followed by worldwide media promotion. “Unity Tunnel” will also be celebrated at the &lt;strong&gt;CODAsummit 2026&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;in Santa Fe, New Mexico where the project will be featured in a video presentation.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h5&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About “Absolute Equality: Unity Tunnel”&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;“Absolute Equality: Unity Tunnel” transforms the 160-foot-long underpass beneath Pleasant Valley Road at Lady Bird Lake into an iconic public artwork, highlighting Austin's culture and heritage heard directly from the voices of over 250 Austin residents. &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;The artwork masterfully features over 689,000 vibrant hand-laid tiles that make up the 1,000 square-foot mosaic mural, over 1,000 square feet of custom flagstone, and 3,000 square feet of a painted ceiling mural. The artwork is by Texas artist, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;a href="https://gcc02.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fus.list-manage.com%2FAQi3QzG7h_8%3Fe%3De70aa4c24c%26c2id%3Dff4227888f0dfd05153fcefbfcf6992e&amp;amp;data=05%7C02%7Cmedia%40austintexas.gov%7Cdd8f198fe3ca4f8bf65008df0872e7cb%7C5c5e19f6a6ab4b45b1d0be4608a9a67f%7C0%7C0%7C639238961968178819%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;amp;sdata=ht4ULca5XDOfaLHDzuSI5w7%2FSJykefCtz7Mbpz3ma%2FQ%3D&amp;amp;reserved=0"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Reginald C. Adams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span&gt;, a visionary public artist and community developer whose work has woven art and civic life together for over 30 years.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About Arts, Culture, Music and Entertainment&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;Austin Arts, Culture, Music, and Entertainment (ACME) is committed to fostering a vibrant, inclusive creative ecosystem that celebrates and supports Austin's artists, musicians, entertainment venues, and cultural institutions. ACME is a centralized hub offering resources, funding opportunities, and strategic programs that promote local talent and drive economic growth.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
  <pubDate>September 2, 2026</pubDate>
    <dc:creator/>
    <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.austintexas.gov/143686</guid>
    </item>
<item>
  <title>Austin's Art in Public Places Artwork "Absolute Equality: Unity Tunnel" Named 2026 CODAworx Winner for Best Collaboration</title>
  <link>https://www.austintexas.gov/communications/news/austins-art-public-places-artwork-absolute-equality-unity-tunnel-named-2026</link>
  <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Austin Arts, Culture, Music and Entertainment’s (ACME) &lt;strong&gt;“Absolute Equality: Unity Tunnel,”&lt;/strong&gt; an iconic Art in Public Places (AIPP) work by Texas artist &lt;strong&gt;Reginald Adams&lt;/strong&gt;, has been selected as the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;a href="https://gcc02.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fus.list-manage.com%2F14rxjyw5YJA%3Fe%3De70aa4c24c%26c2id%3Dff4227888f0dfd05153fcefbfcf6992e&amp;amp;data=05%7C02%7Cmedia%40austintexas.gov%7Cdd8f198fe3ca4f8bf65008df0872e7cb%7C5c5e19f6a6ab4b45b1d0be4608a9a67f%7C0%7C0%7C639238961968160460%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;amp;sdata=7Jecziz7iaZaK0d3%2Ft9%2FPNIy5fyMtCTdTsxtD5TGn%2F8%3D&amp;amp;reserved=0"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2026 CODAworx Winner for Best Collaboration&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;The &lt;/span&gt;&lt;a href="https://gcc02.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fus.list-manage.com%2FmZD2RTrNNnV%3Fe%3De70aa4c24c%26c2id%3Dff4227888f0dfd05153fcefbfcf6992e&amp;amp;data=05%7C02%7Cmedia%40austintexas.gov%7Cdd8f198fe3ca4f8bf65008df0872e7cb%7C5c5e19f6a6ab4b45b1d0be4608a9a67f%7C0%7C0%7C639238961968169704%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;amp;sdata=G%2B%2FLjjmZsTCKV9UKcAqP4bsneCctySTry%2FEIjymvRV4%3D&amp;amp;reserved=0"&gt;&lt;span&gt;CODAawards&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span&gt; honor excellence in commissioned art around the world. This year’s jurors were particularly impressed by the collaborative spirit behind “Unity Tunnel,” describing the project as “the very definition of collaboration.” This recognition celebrates the partnership among AIPP, Adams, Austin Transportation and Public Works, Austin Capital Delivery Services, HDR Inc., Jay-Reese Contractors, Inc., Austin Parks and Recreation, local non-profit The Trail Conservancy, and the over 250 Austin residents who shaped every stage of its development.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;From CODAworx - “This year's competition drew 302 submissions from 19 countries, collectively representing more than $123 million in commissions to artists. A distinguished panel of jurors from the fields of art, architecture, and design selected winners across twelve categories spanning sculpture, murals, light art, and beyond — recognizing the creative professionals whose work transforms environments, shapes experiences, and strengthens community connection."&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h5&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A Triumph of Community Partnership&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;“Unity Tunnel” was created through an extensive community engagement process designed to ensure that Austin residents had a meaningful role in shaping the artwork. &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;From early community surveys and engagements to hands-on workshops at the Mendez Recreation Center and The Mosaic Workshop, AIPP and Adams worked alongside community members to develop “Unity Tunnel” with broad and meaningful public participation.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;The project took innovative approaches to building trust for community participation. Community activities included sound baths, guided meditation, and quiet reflection, helped ensure that voices across all ages and backgrounds were included, inspiring community pride and shaping an artwork meant to endure for generations.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h5&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A Celebrated Moment for Austin’s Public Art Program&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;“This recognition shines a light on the power of collaboration in public art,” said &lt;strong&gt;Angela Means, ACME Director.&lt;/strong&gt; “’Unity Tunnel’ is a testament to what’s possible when artists, residents, and City partners come together with imagination, dedication, and heart.”&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;CODAworx is scheduled to publicly announce the 2026 award winners on &lt;strong&gt;Monday, August 31,&lt;/strong&gt; followed by worldwide media promotion. “Unity Tunnel” will also be celebrated at the &lt;strong&gt;CODAsummit 2026&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;in Santa Fe, New Mexico where the project will be featured in a video presentation.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h5&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About “Absolute Equality: Unity Tunnel”&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;“Absolute Equality: Unity Tunnel” transforms the 160-foot-long underpass beneath Pleasant Valley Road at Lady Bird Lake into an iconic public artwork, highlighting Austin's culture and heritage heard directly from the voices of over 250 Austin residents. &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;The artwork masterfully features over 689,000 vibrant hand-laid tiles that make up the 1,000 square-foot mosaic mural, over 1,000 square feet of custom flagstone, and 3,000 square feet of a painted ceiling mural. The artwork is by Texas artist, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;a href="https://gcc02.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fus.list-manage.com%2FAQi3QzG7h_8%3Fe%3De70aa4c24c%26c2id%3Dff4227888f0dfd05153fcefbfcf6992e&amp;amp;data=05%7C02%7Cmedia%40austintexas.gov%7Cdd8f198fe3ca4f8bf65008df0872e7cb%7C5c5e19f6a6ab4b45b1d0be4608a9a67f%7C0%7C0%7C639238961968178819%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;amp;sdata=ht4ULca5XDOfaLHDzuSI5w7%2FSJykefCtz7Mbpz3ma%2FQ%3D&amp;amp;reserved=0"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Reginald C. Adams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span&gt;, a visionary public artist and community developer whose work has woven art and civic life together for over 30 years.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About Arts, Culture, Music and Entertainment&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;Austin Arts, Culture, Music, and Entertainment (ACME) is committed to fostering a vibrant, inclusive creative ecosystem that celebrates and supports Austin's artists, musicians, entertainment venues, and cultural institutions. ACME is a centralized hub offering resources, funding opportunities, and strategic programs that promote local talent and drive economic growth.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
  <pubDate>September 2, 2026</pubDate>
